1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Moving on to congestive heart failure,

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,500
this is when the heart fails to pump appropriately.

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:08,000
It's divided into right and left-sided failure.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,500
Some causes of left-sided failure include ischemia.

5
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:12,500
If you decrease the blood flow to the heart,

6
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,000
that would damage the myocardium,

7
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
and hence the myocardium would not function properly.

8
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:18,000
A hypertension can do it.

9
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
The idea is that when you have hypertension,

10
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
you get concentric left ventricular hypertrophy.

11
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
As there's an increased stress on the heart,

12
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,500
the heart is going to undergo hypertrophy

13
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:29,500
in order to be able to deal with that stress.

14
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,500
Now, the more that you get hypertrophy of the wall of the heart,

15
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,500
the harder it is to be able to oxygenate that wall,

16
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:37,500
because, of course, if this is the normal thickness of the wall,

17
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:39,500
and now you make the wall this thick,

18
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:43,500
you've got to be able to oxygenate all of this extra mass of muscle.

19
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,500
And so the consequence of long-standing hypertension with hypertrophy

20
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,500
is that eventually you start getting ischemic damage to the heart,

21
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,500
which then eventually can result in a left-sided heart failure.

22
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:54,500
Dilated cardiomyopathy is a disorder

23
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:57,500
in which you get four-chamber dilatation of the heart,

24
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:00,500
left and right atrium and left and right ventricle.

25
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,500
And as you get dilatation, you're going to stretch the muscle,

26
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:04,500
and as you stretch the muscle,

27
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:06,500
it's not going to be able to contract as well.

28
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:09,500
And so that would be another cause of left-sided failure.

29
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:12,500
Myocardial infarction, of course, if you infarct the myocardium,

30
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:14,500
it's not going to be functioning.

31
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:16,500
And so that can result in a left-sided failure.

32
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,500
And finally, restrictive cardiomyopathy is a disorder

33
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:21,500
where you can't fill the heart appropriately.

34
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:23,500
And of course, if you can't fill the heart,

35
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:25,500
you're not going to be able to pump blood appropriately.

36
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:27,500
And so you'll get a left-sided failure.

37
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,500
So these are some of the important causes of left-sided failure.

38
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,500
The clinical consequences of left-sided heart failure

39
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:35,500
will be predominantly based on pulmonary congestion.

40
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:37,500
When the heart can't pump the blood forward,

41
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:39,500
it's going to pile up backward,

42
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,500
and it's going to start piling up within the blood vessels of the lung,

43
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:44,500
i.e. pulmonary congestion.

44
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:48,500
Now this will increase the hydrostatic pressure within the blood vessels,

45
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:50,500
leading to pulmonary edema with dyspnea.

46
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,500
In addition, they'll have paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea,

47
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:57,500
which is where they get dyspnea as they lay flat over a period of hours.

48
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,500
They'll have orthopnea, where they get dyspnea

49
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:02,500
if they lay flat over a period of just a few minutes.

50
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:04,500
In addition, they'll have crackles,

51
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:06,500
which represents the edema or the fluid

52
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:08,500
within the interstitial space of the lung.

53
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:10,500
Another consequence of pulmonary congestion

54
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:13,500
will be that there will be rupture of some of the capillaries

55
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:14,500
that are engorged.

56
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:16,500
Now when these capillaries rupture,

57
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:19,500
blood will then leak from the capillaries into the alveolar air sacs,

58
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:23,500
and the macrophages of the alveolar air sacs will consume that blood.

59
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:24,500
As they consume that blood,

60
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:26,500
they're going to be consuming a ton of iron,

61
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:29,500
and so iron will begin to pile up within those macrophages,

62
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:32,500
and those are called hemocytorin-laden macrophages,

63
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:35,500
and also known as heart failure cells.

64
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:37,500
Here's a picture of the hemocytorin-laden macrophages.

65
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:39,500
This is the alveolar air sac,

66
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:41,500
and you can see that you've got these macrophages

67
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:44,500
within the alveolar air sac that are loaded with hemocytorin.

68
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:49,500
Again, hemocytorin-laden macrophages are also known as heart failure cells.

69
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:52,500
An additional consequence of left-sided heart failure

70
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:55,500
will be that there will be decreased forward perfusion.

71
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:58,500
Perhaps the greatest consequence of this decreased forward perfusion

72
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:02,500
will be that you'll get activation of the renin-angiotensin system.

73
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:05,500
Recall that if there is decreased blood flow to the kidneys,

74
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:09,500
the juxtaglomerular apparatus will get activated, releasing renin.

75
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,500
Renin will then convert angiotensinogen to angiotensin I.

76
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,500
Angiotensin I will get converted to angiotensin II

77
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:17,500
by angiotensin-converting enzyme.

78
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,500
Now, angiotensin II will then do two things.

79
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:22,500
Number one, it'll constrict the arterioles,

80
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:24,500
increasing the total peripheral resistance.

81
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:26,500
And number two, it'll go to the adrenal gland,

82
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:29,500
causing release of aldosterone, which will come back to the kidney,

83
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,500
cause resorption of sodium,

84
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:35,500
and eventually water will follow so that you increase the blood volume.

85
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:37,500
Now, imagine if a patient has congestive heart failure

86
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:40,500
and you increase the total peripheral resistance,

87
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:42,500
you're just going to exacerbate the failure.

88
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:45,500
In addition, if the patient has congestive heart failure

89
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:47,500
and you increase the blood volume,

90
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,500
that's only further going to, again, exacerbate the problem.

91
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:54,500
And so this results in a downward spiral of the congestive heart failure.

92
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:57,500
And that's why one of the mainstays of treatment of congestive heart failure

93
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:00,500
is an ACE inhibitor, and that's particularly high yield.

94
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:02,500
Now, moving on to the right side,

95
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:05,500
right-sided heart failure is most commonly due to left failure.

96
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:07,500
Illustrating this is this is the left heart

97
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:09,500
and it's draining the pulmonary circuit.

98
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,500
Then it's important to know that the right heart

99
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:14,500
is actually feeding the pulmonary circuit.

100
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:18,500
And so, if there is backup of blood into the pulmonary circuit,

101
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:20,500
eventually that would back up into the right heart

102
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:22,500
and then you'd get a right heart failure.

103
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:24,500
And so, again, very high yield to know

104
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,500
that the most common cause of right-sided heart failure

105
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:29,500
is actually left-sided failure.

106
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:31,500
Other important causes of right-sided failure

107
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:33,500
include left to right shunts.

108
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:35,500
If you put extra blood into the right side,

109
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:37,500
for example, with a shunt,

110
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:39,500
so if this is right atrium, right ventricle,

111
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:41,500
left atrium, left ventricle,

112
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:43,500
if there is a shunt from, for example,

113
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:45,500
the left ventricle to the right ventricle,

114
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:47,500
that's going to then increase the amount of blood

115
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:49,500
that goes into the right ventricle,

116
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:51,500
increasing the risk for right heart failure.

117
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:54,500
And then finally, chronic lung disease can also result

118
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:56,500
in a right-sided heart failure.

119
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:58,500
The idea here is that when you have lung disease,

120
00:04:58,500 --> 00:04:59,500
you develop hypoxia.

121
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:01,500
And one of the consequences of hypoxia

122
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:03,500
is that the blood vessels in the lung

123
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:06,500
will constrict in response to that hypoxia.

124
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:09,500
Now, imagine if you have hypoxia throughout the lung,

125
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,500
then the blood vessels of the entire lung

126
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:12,500
are going to constrict,

127
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:14,500
and the right heart will have to pump

128
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:16,500
against those constricted blood vessels.

129
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:18,500
Eventually, the right heart will fail,

130
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:20,500
and that's called core pulmonale.

131
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:23,500
Now, the clinical features of right-sided heart failure

132
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:25,500
are going to be due to congestion

133
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:27,500
of those things that feed the right heart.

134
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,500
So, of course, the jugular vein feeds into the right heart,

135
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:32,500
so the patients can get jugular venous distension.

136
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:35,500
Of course, the venous system coming from the liver

137
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:38,500
and the spleen also feeds into the right heart,

138
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:41,500
and so the patients can develop painful hepatosplenomegaly.

139
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:44,500
One of the consequences of long-standing congestion

140
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:46,500
in the liver would be cirrhosis,

141
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:49,500
and when that occurs, we call that cardiac cirrhosis.

142
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:51,500
Finally, because the lower extremities are also draining

143
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:52,500
into the right heart,

144
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:54,500
you'll get increased hydrostatic pressure

145
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:55,500
within the lower extremities,

146
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:58,500
resulting in a dependent pitting edema.

147
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:00,500
Here's a picture of a congested liver.

148
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:02,500
Recall that when you look at the surface of a normal liver,

149
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:05,500
the color should be absolutely equal across the liver.

150
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:08,500
However, note here that we've got all these splotches of red

151
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:12,500
throughout the liver representing congested central veins.

152
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:16,500
This then creates this variegated color to the surface of the liver

153
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:18,500
which resembles a nutmeg,

154
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:20,500
and so this is also called the nutmeg liver.

155
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:23,500
And that concludes our section on congestive heart failure.

